---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import PageCards from "../../components/PageCards.astro";
import { nav } from "../../lib/nav";
import { url } from "../../lib/url";

const section = nav.find((s) => s.href === "/cicd/")!;
---

<Base
    title="CI setup"
    description="Wire ci-doctor into GitLab CI, GitHub Actions, or an air-gapped network."
>
    <h1>CI setup</h1>
    <p class="lead">
        Wire it into the <em>post</em> stage so it explains failures without ever
        changing the outcome. Pick your pipeline:
    </p>

    <PageCards pages={section.pages} />

    <h2>The shape is the same everywhere</h2>
    <ul class="tight">
        <li>
            <strong>Runs only on failure.</strong> A post-stage job gated on the pipeline's
            result, not a step in your build.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Read-only, and always exits 0.</strong> Adding it cannot fail
            your pipeline or mask the real error.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Needs one read-scoped token</strong> to fetch the failed run's
            logs — and nothing else.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>The model is optional.</strong> Without one you get the deterministic
            report, not a stub.
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        What differs between providers is only where the run id comes from and
        how a note gets posted back. <a href={url("/start/requirements/")}
            >Requirements</a
        > covers the token scopes; <a href={url("/reference/configuration/")}
            >Configuration</a
        > covers every key you can set from the job.
    </p>
</Base>
